{
  "title": "Application Monitoring, Logging, and Error Management",
  "introduction": "This document outlines the comprehensive strategy for application monitoring, logging, and error management within the merged software cluster. The system integrates multiple specialized tools—Sentry for error tracking and performance monitoring, Mnemosyne for distributed tracing, and Telegraf for custom metrics collection—to provide deep visibility into application health, performance bottlenecks, and error occurrences across both monolithic and microservice architectures. This multi-faceted approach ensures robust observability and efficient incident response.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Core Monitoring Solutions",
      "content": [
        {
          "heading": "Sentry: Error Tracking and Performance Monitoring",
          "description": "Sentry serves as the primary solution for error tracking and performance monitoring across the application stack. Its configuration is meticulously defined to optimize data collection and relevance:",
          "details": [
            "**In-App Stack Traces**: Sentry is configured to identify code within `api`, `app`, `config` (excluding `acfs.rb`), and `lib` (excluding `acfs_rails_cache.rb`) as 'in-app' code, focusing stack traces on relevant application logic. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb]",
            "**Excluded Exceptions**: A predefined list of common and expected exceptions, including various `Acfs` and `Restify` gateway errors, `Status::NotFound`, `ApplicationJob::ExpectedRetry`, and `Redis` connection errors, are explicitly filtered to reduce noise in error reports. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb]",
            "**Reduced Payload**: The `send_modules` option is set to `false` to minimize data payload size by preventing the transmission of a full list of installed gems with each event. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb]",
            "**Parameter Filtering**: A `before_send` hook leverages `ActiveSupport::ParameterFilter` to sanitize event data, ensuring sensitive parameters (as defined in `Rails.application.config.filter_parameters`) are not transmitted. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb]",
            "**Performance Tracing**: Transaction tracing is enabled with a configurable `traces_sample_rate` (defaulting to `1.0` via `ENV['SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE']`), alongside profile sampling at `1.0`. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb]",
            "**Dynamic Sampling**: A `traces_sampler` lambda provides granular control, respecting parent sampling decisions, ignoring health check endpoints (`/up`, `/ping`, `/system_info`), and applying the default sample rate otherwise. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb]",
            "**Global Tags**: Sentry events are globally tagged with `site` and `brand` values derived from `Xikolo.site` and `Xikolo.brand` for enhanced categorization and filtering. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb]",
            "**Web and Microservice Configurations**: Sentry configurations exist for both the main web application and individual microservices, ensuring consistent error and performance monitoring across the entire architecture. [Source: C05_Monitoring_Observability]"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Mnemosyne: Distributed Tracing",
          "description": "Mnemosyne is integrated for distributed tracing, providing end-to-end visibility into request flows across services.",
          "details": [
            "**AMQP Sink**: Trace data is sent to an AMQP sink, specifically RabbitMQ, with the endpoint defaulting to `amqp://localhost` or `ENV['XIKOLO_RABBITMQ_URL']`. [Source: config/mnemosyne.yml]",
            "**Application Identification**: The application is identified as `web` within Mnemosyne traces. [Source: config/mnemosyne.yml]",
            "**Environment-Specific Disabling**: Mnemosyne is explicitly disabled for `test` and `integration` environments to optimize resource usage during development and testing. [Source: config/mnemosyne.yml]",
            "**Mnemosyne Configuration**: Comprehensive configuration for Mnemosyne is managed to ensure proper trace data collection and routing. [Source: C05_Monitoring_Observability]"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Telegraf: Custom Metrics Collection",
          "description": "Custom application metrics are collected using Telegraf, facilitated by the `xikolo-common` gem's `Metrics` module.",
          "details": [
            "**Metrics Agent**: An instance of `::Telegraf::Agent` is initialized to send UDP packets to `localhost:8094`, expecting a local Telegraf agent to receive and forward these metrics. [Source: gems/xikolo-common/lib/xikolo/common/metrics.rb]",
            "**Application-Wide Access**: The `Xikolo` module is extended with this `Metrics` functionality, making `Xikolo.metrics` available for emitting custom metrics throughout the application code. [Source: gems/xikolo-common/lib/xikolo/common/metrics.rb]"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Key Integrations and Correlation",
      "content": [
        {
          "heading": "Sentry-Mnemosyne Correlation (`AnnotateSentryErrorsForMnemosyne`)",
          "description": "A custom Rack middleware, `AnnotateSentryErrorsForMnemosyne`, is crucial for correlating Sentry errors with Mnemosyne traces. When an active Mnemosyne trace is detected, its UUID is added to the current Sentry scope as `mnemosyne.trace_id`. This enables developers to seamlessly navigate from an error in Sentry to its corresponding distributed performance trace in Mnemosyne, significantly aiding in root cause analysis. This middleware is a key component mentioned across all analysis sources. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb, monitoring_logging_error_handling, C05_Monitoring_Observability]"
        },
        {
          "heading": "MsgrSentryIntegration for Microservices",
          "description": "For microservices, a specific `MsgrSentryIntegration` component is utilized. This integration likely handles Sentry's interaction with message-based communication patterns common in microservice architectures, ensuring that errors and performance data from inter-service communication are also captured and correlated effectively. [Source: C05_Monitoring_Observability]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Error Management and Filtering",
      "content": [
        {
          "heading": "Sensitive Parameter Filtering",
          "description": "`ActiveSupport::ParameterFilter` is consistently applied to sanitize sensitive data before it is sent to monitoring systems, upholding data privacy and security standards. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb, monitoring_logging_error_handling]"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Application-Level Error Handling (`ErrorsController`)",
          "description": "Beyond automated error tracking, an `ErrorsController` is in place, likely responsible for handling and rendering user-friendly error pages for various HTTP status codes, providing a graceful degradation experience for end-users. [Source: monitoring_logging_error_handling]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Workflows",
      "content": [
        {
          "heading": "1. Error Reporting Workflow",
          "description": "Unhandled exceptions within the application are captured by Sentry. Before transmission, sensitive parameters are filtered, and a predefined list of common exceptions is ignored to reduce noise. The `AnnotateSentryErrorsForMnemosyne` middleware ensures that the current Mnemosyne trace ID is attached to the Sentry event, enabling direct correlation. For user-facing errors, the `ErrorsController` provides appropriate responses. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb, monitoring_logging_error_handling]"
        },
        {
          "heading": "2. Performance Tracing Workflow",
          "description": "Sentry collects performance data for HTTP requests and background jobs, applying dynamic sampling based on configured rates and rules (e.g., ignoring health checks). Concurrently, Mnemosyne collects detailed distributed traces, which are then asynchronously sent to a RabbitMQ instance via AMQP. [Source: config/initializers/sentry.rb, config/mnemosyne.yml]"
        },
        {
          "heading": "3. Custom Metrics Collection Workflow",
          "description": "Application code can emit custom metrics (e.g., counters, gauges) using the `Xikolo.metrics` interface. These metrics are sent via UDP to a local Telegraf agent, which is responsible for aggregating and forwarding them to a time-series database for visualization and alerting. [Source: gems/xikolo-common/lib/xikolo/common/metrics.rb]"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "conclusion": "The merged cluster employs a robust and integrated monitoring, logging, and error management strategy. By combining the strengths of Sentry, Mnemosyne, and Telegraf, alongside custom integrations like `AnnotateSentryErrorsForMnemosyne` and `MsgrSentryIntegration`, the system achieves comprehensive observability across its monolithic and microservice components. This unified approach facilitates proactive issue detection, efficient debugging, and continuous performance optimization."
}

